{
  "families": [
    {
      "budget_share": 0.45,
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        { "choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding" },
        { "choices": null, "high": 1, "low": -2, "param": "angle_guard" },
        { "choices": null, "high": 3, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard" },
        { "choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 13, "param": "data_width" },
        { "choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 10, "param": "n_iter" },
        { "choices": null, "high": 8, "low": 2, "param": "m" }
      ],
      "why": "Most likely low-power winner AND trivially feasible on throughput. It emits 1 result/cycle, so the 50 MSPS floor is met at any Fmax >= ~50 MHz, and latency is unconstrained by the spec. Registering only every m stages cuts flip-flop count/area versus full pipelined, and since we do not need high Fmax, large m (fewer, deeper stages) is the natural direction; m is left 2..8 so the search can trace the whole area/depth trade-off. data_width 13..20 and n_iter 10..20 bracket the 2^-12 accuracy boundary (13 bits of output alone are needed before CORDIC residual error) up to comfortable margin, and the guard fields let the search trade bits for cells."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 0.30,
      "family": "pipelined",
      "ranges": [
        { "choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding" },
        { "choices": null, "high": 1, "low": -2, "param": "angle_guard" },
        { "choices": null, "high": 3, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard" },
        { "choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 13, "param": "data_width" },
        { "choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 10, "param": "n_iter" }
      ],
      "why": "The reference full-pipeline family: 1 result/cycle so the 50 MSPS constraint is met at essentially any sky130 Fmax, giving the high-Fmax / high-power anchor of the front. It is the family we must beat on power, so it must be mapped. Same accuracy ranges as pipelined_m (accuracy depends only on data_width/n_iter/angle_guard/frac_guard/rounding, and all families are bit-identical), which also makes it a clean control for attributing power differences to the register structure."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 0.15,
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        { "choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding" },
        { "choices": null, "high": 1, "low": -2, "param": "angle_guard" },
        { "choices": null, "high": 3, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard" },
        { "choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 13, "param": "data_width" },
        { "choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 10, "param": "n_iter" },
        { "choices": null, "high": 8, "low": 4, "param": "k" }
      ],
      "why": "Shares one datapath/FSM so it can have a lower area/power floor than the fully registered pipeline, but it only produces a result every ceil(N/k)+3 cycles, so feasibility demands Fmax >= 50*(ceil(N/k)+3). For the N ~ 12-18 needed for 2^-12 that only has a chance at large k (k>=4, realistically 6-8), so k is biased high. Worth a modest share: if it lands on the front it beats pipelined on area, and if it cannot clear 50 MSPS at feasible N that is itself decisive information."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 0.10,
      "family": "iterative",
      "ranges": [
        { "choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding" },
        { "choices": null, "high": 1, "low": -2, "param": "angle_guard" },
        { "choices": null, "high": 3, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard" },
        { "choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 13, "param": "data_width" },
        { "choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 10, "param": "n_iter" }
      ],
      "why": "Smallest area and the power reference, but it takes N+3 cycles/result, so 50 MSPS requires Fmax >= 50*(N+3): ~700-900+ MHz at the N ~ 12-18 that 2^-12 needs, which is very unlikely in sky130_fd_sc_hd. Given a small but non-zero budget purely to confirm/deny feasibility with real numbers rather than assume it, and to keep it on record in case the model finds an unexpected high-Fmax corner. If it cannot clear the throughput floor across its range it will drop out of later rounds."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The binding constraints are throughput >= 50 MSPS together with max_abs_err <= 2^-12 (i.e. accuracy_bits >= 12). Accuracy is family-independent (identical compute bits), so it only sets the (data_width, n_iter, guards) window: roughly data_width >= 13 and n_iter ~ 10-20 to clear the output-quantisation and CORDIC-residual floors, with guard bits as the fine trade-off knob. That leaves throughput and power as the family/parameter discriminators. Families that emit 1 result/cycle (pipelined, pipelined_m) meet 50 MSPS at any plausible sky130 Fmax and are therefore the safe feasible core; families with a multi-cycle result rate (unrolled_k, iterative) only qualify at high Fmax and so need large k / small N+3, which is exactly why their budgets are smaller and their k is biased high. Since latency is unconstrained, pipelined_m's depth-vs-register trade-off (large m) is the most promising low-power region, so it gets the largest share, with pipelined as the high-Fmax anchor and control. Round 1 (100 evals) is split pipelined_m 45 / pipelined 30 / unrolled_k 15 / iterative 10; the same proportions carry into the run's 400-eval budget. If iterative and unrolled_k turn out unable to reach 50 MSPS at feasible N they will be abandoned and their share moved onto the winning family, including the reserved end-of-run full-range mapping pass."
}

{
  "rationale": "Only pipelined and pipelined_m can ever satisfy throughput>=50 MSPS: iterative topped out at 9.83 MSPS and unrolled_k at 5.84 MSPS over their evaluations, so both are structurally infeasible here and should not consume budget. The feasible front is only 3 unique points and is badly under-mapped on the axis that matters most for the selection rule (min power_index): pipelined_m owns the low-power corner (power_index=2.56) yet produced just 1 feasible design in 45 evals, inside a razor-thin feasible box (data_width=19, n_iter=15, angle_guard=1, frac_guard=0, m=2) — clearly the m=2..8 / other-width low-power region is unexplored. Meanwhile pipelined's feasible points cluster tightly at power_index 5.36-5.43 with accuracy 15.0-15.6 bits. The covered power range 2.56..5.43 is a small fraction of the HV reference (40), and accuracy 13.5..15.6 sits well above the 12-bit reference, so hypervolume is being left on the table at the low-power end. This is exactly the 'front covers only a small part of an objective's range' case: spend this round letting NSGA-II map the whole front over the full ranges of the two viable families (pipelined, pipelined_m), seeded with the current front, rather than refining a single narrow box.",
  "next_plan": null,
  "decision": "map_front"
}

{
  "decision": "map_front",
  "rationale": "Only pipelined and pipelined_m are feasible: iterative (10 evals, 0 feasible, max 9.83 MSPS) and unrolled_k (15 evals, 0 feasible, max 5.84 MSPS) cannot reach the 50 MSPS constraint, so the front lives entirely on the two pipelined families. The current front spans power_index 2.56..9.55 and accuracy_bits 13.5..22.7, but the HV reference point is at power_index 40, so the front covers only a small part of the power objective's range and has visible gaps (3.96->5.69, 6.0->8.45). The best accuracy seen anywhere is 22.84 bits vs the front's 22.7, and n_iter on the front only reaches 28 (registry allows 30), so the high-accuracy end is not yet saturated. With 200 of 400 evals remaining, the right move is to let code run NSGA-II over the full ranges of pipelined and pipelined_m seeded with the current front, filling the gaps and extending both ends of the trade-off curve before the final selection (min power_index) is made."
  ,
  "next_plan": null
}

{
  "rationale": "Budget and round cap are both exhausted (400/400 evals, round 4 of 4), and the spec is demonstrably satisfiable, so `infeasible` is not warranted. The feasible front holds 239 designs (202 unique) across all four families, with hypervolume 429.1 and a further +8.2% gain this round, so the search has converged rather than stalled. Both constraints are met with margin: throughput reaches 180 MSPS vs the 50 MSPS floor, and max_abs_err reaches 2^-24.46 vs the 2^-12 limit. The front spans power_index 2.56..11 against the HV reference 40 and accuracy_bits 13.5..24.5 against the reference 12, i.e. it covers a wide slice of both objective ranges, and data_width on the front spans 18..28 of the registry's 8..28. The selection rule (min power_index) is already served by the front's low-power corner: pipelined_m [data_width=19 n_iter=15 angle_guard=1 frac_guard=0 rounding=round m=2] at power_index=2.56, accuracy_bits=13.5 (max_abs_err=8.66e-05, comfortably under 2^-12), 72.3 MSPS, 35202 um^2, 549 FFs. iterative and unrolled_k are correctly excluded: 0 feasible designs each, with max throughput 9.83 and 5.84 MSPS respectively, far below the 50 MSPS requirement. No further evaluation budget exists to refine or map, so stopping is the correct terminal decision.",
  "next_plan": null
  ,"decision": "stop"
}
